body:
Having tried to [implement this in chromium](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/7098960), I've come across a problem with using `#wait-for-all`: in any case where `intercept()` is "done" synchronously (no `handler`s, a `handler` rejects immediately, or all `handler`s resolve immediately), there's an observable inversion beween `committed` promises and `navigatesuccess`/`navigateerror`.

For example:
```
navigation.onnavigate = e => e.intercept();
navigation.onnavigatesuccess = () => console.log("navigatesucceess");
let result = navigation.navigate("#");
result.committed.then(() => console.log("committed"));
```

The current chromium behavior will log `committed` then `navigatesuccess`. However, using `#wait-for-all` inverts that, because we `#wait-for-all` inside `navigation.navigate()` and therefore the `#wait-for-all` reaction gets enqueued before JS has  a chance to call `commited.then()`.

It's not clear to me whether this is a dealbreaker, but it surprised me when I tried to implement `#wait-for-all`, and more tests broke than just the ones asserting a specific order (e.g., several tests in `navigation-api/focus-reset`, which assume that [potentially resetting the focus](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#potentially-reset-the-focus) cannot have happened already when awaiting the `committed` promise).

> That's very interesting. We ran into a similar issue when implementing #inner-navigate-event-firing-algorithm, where we needed to [delay the call to #wait-for-all](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/rev/0516e0135dab129510c0dee588ea75ffa62f24c8/dom/navigation/Navigation.cpp#1386) in case there was an ongoing method tracker. We thought this was a quirk with Gecko, and tried to follow the intent of the spec. If Blink also suffers from this I start learning towards something being underspecced. This is another spec issue, not related to this one.

I think it *is* related to this one, and to the fact that this API mixes events and promises.

> If there is a requirement that onsuccess should fire after reactions to the committed promise this should be made explicit in spec. I think onerror doesn't have this requirement, so possibly Gecko's solution isn't entirely optional and only the success callback should be a reaction to the committed promise, if such exists. Implicitly betting that the extra lap on the microtask queue fixes this is not a solution, especially for fast failure handling and non-method tracker navigations.

I am still a bit tentative about "fast failure handling" here and why this matters.
These failures mostly arise from 

Usually when you want to respond to a failure in a list of promises you do something like this:

```js
// "get a promise waiting for all"
await Promise.all(handlers).catch(handle_failure)
```

And not something like this where some cases have a :
```js
function wait_for_all(handlers, on_success, on_failure) {
  if (!handlers.size) {
    queueMicrotask(on_success);
    return;
  }

  let pending_count = handlers.length;
    for (const handler of handlers) {
       handler.catch(on_failure).then(() => { 
         if (--pending_count)
           on_success();
    }
  });
}
```

> 
> In the end these order issues are an inherent problem in the difference in nature between events and promises.

Exactly.
All of these workflows in the navigation API can both respond to promises and emit promises/events.

I think they should look like this, and I find everything else is harder to explain:

```js
function handle_navigate_event(precommit_handlers, handlers, abort_signal) {

  const transition_committed = Promise.withResolvers();
  const transition_finished = Promise.withResolvers();
  const method_tracker_committed = Promise.withResolvers();
  const method_tracker_finished = Promise.withResolvers();

  (async () => {
    try {
      if (precommit_handlers.length)
        await Promise.all(precommit_handlers);

      abort_signal.throwIfAborted();

      method_tracker_committed.resolve();
      transition_committed.resolve();

      await Promise.all(handlers);

      abort_signal.throwIfAborted();
   } catch (reason) {
      method_tracker_committed.reject(reason);
      method_tracker_finished.reject(reason);
      navigation.dispatchEvent("navigateerror", reason);
      transition_committed.reject(reason);
      transition_finished.reject(reason);
   } finally {
      method_tracker_finished.resolve();
      navigation.dispatchEvent("navigatesuccess");
      transition_finished.resolve();
   }
  })();

  return { committed: method_tracker_committed, finished: method_tracker_finished };
}
```


> I'd argue that using #wait-for-all produces the most consistent timing behavior, as [@smaug----](https://github.com/smaug----) notes. The fact that something that immediately rejects gets scheduled after something that hasn't even been scheduled yet is very surprising.

So this is surprising?
```js
Promise.all([Promise.reject("error")]).catch(e => console.log(e));
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("abc"))
```

The above would log "abc" before "error", even though the "abc" microtask was scheduled after the "immediate" rejection. 
I think we should follow this cow path here (`get a promise waiting for all`, or `Promise.all([...])` ) rather than make the exotic `wait for all` web exposed.

So to make it clear, we're not making `#wait-for-all` web exposed. It is just an implementation detail in the spec. It is uses exactly because it does not schedule an extra microtask.  It is because I don't find `Promise.all` to be surprising that I think that it's a bad fit here. The issue is that it makes _all_ navigation behave as if they were asynchronous, which they aren't. Just consider setting `location.hash`. What would you expect from the following:

```javascript
  const events = [];

  addEventListener("hashchange", () => {
    events.push("hashchange");
  });

  navigation.addEventListener("navigatesuccess", () => {
    events.push("navigatesuccess");
  });

  navigation.addEventListener("navigateerror", () => {
    events.push("navigateerror");
  });

  let ctr = 0;
  location.hash = `#${ctr++}`;
  Promise.resolve().then(() => (location.hash = `#${ctr++}`));
```
I expect `["navigatesuccess", "navigatesuccess", "hashchange", "hashchange"]`, which is what Gecko does. Chrome gives `["navigatesuccess", "navigateerror", "hashchange", "hashchange"]`. And yes, this is very weird. Especially when you consider history. The session history in Gecko is `["/", "#0", "#1"]`, and the session history in Blink is also `["/", "#0", "#1"]`. And this I find weird.

Not using `#wait-for-all` breaks synchronous navigations.

EDIT: I also checked `navigation.entries()`, and Gecko and Blink agree that there should be three entries `["/", "#0", "#1"]`. Even when Blink said that the middle one failed.

> So to make it clear, we're not making `#wait-for-all` web exposed. It is just an implementation detail in the spec. 

I get that, poor wording. I meant making the difference between "wait for all" and `Promise.all` web observable.

