> 
>   const events = [];
> 
>   addEventListener("hashchange", () => {
>     events.push("hashchange");
>   });
> 
>   navigation.addEventListener("navigatesuccess", () => {
>     events.push("navigatesuccess");
>   });
> 
>   navigation.addEventListener("navigateerror", () => {
>     events.push("navigateerror");
>   });
> 
>   let ctr = 0;
>   location.hash = `#${ctr++}`;
>   Promise.resolve().then(() => (location.hash = `#${ctr++}`));
> I expect `["navigatesuccess", "navigatesuccess", "hashchange", "hashchange"]`, which is what Gecko does. Chrome gives `["navigatesuccess", "navigateerror", "hashchange", "hashchange"]`. And yes, this is very weird. Especially when you consider history. The session history in Gecko is `["/", "#0", "#1"]`, and the session history in Blink is also `["/", "#0", "#1"]`. And this I find weird.

I wonder if the thing we need to remove here is actually the dummy promise when the handler list is empty... that's the thing that adds the strange extra microtask and makes a synchronous navigation become async. `Promise.all([])` should be exactly one microtask, unlike `Promise.all([Promise.resolve()])`. 

This way your scenario here would work, and in the intercepted case `committed` would still resolve before `navigatesuccess` / `navigateerror` are fired.

OTOH, when you think about navigations being synchronous in some cases, having `navigatesuccess` firing before `committed` can be reasoned about (they actually fire at the same time but `.then` is one microtask "late").

I would love some more eyes on this though as we'll be playing with the order of something that's already shipped.
